  <wpt lon="-111.50571584701538" lat="31.571922297672476">
    <name>Buenos Aires National Wildlife Refuge</name>
    <cmt>The U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service manages Buenos Aires National Wildlife Refuge, which spans 117,464 acres. Within the refuge are several distinct groups of plants and animals, known as biotic communities, that depend on each other. Visitors will enjoy the semidesert grasslands that blend into the cottonwoods and willow that line riverbanks and wetlands within the refuge.

Initially used for cattle ranching, the land of BANW was purchased by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service as a national wildlife refuge for the endangered masked bobwhite quail in 1985. Since then, refuge staff, wildlife biologists, volunteers, and many partners have worked together to restore the original landscape of open, semidesert grasslands and bring back the native wildlife that once called these lush prairies home.
</cmt>
    <desc>The U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service manages Buenos Aires National Wildlife Refuge, which spans 117,464 acres. Within the refuge are several distinct groups of plants and animals, known as biotic communities, that depend on each other. Visitors will enjoy the semidesert grasslands that blend into the cottonwoods and willow that line riverbanks and wetlands within the refuge.

Initially used for cattle ranching, the land of BANW was purchased by the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service as a national wildlife refuge for the endangered masked bobwhite quail in 1985. Since then, refuge staff, wildlife biologists, volunteers, and many partners have worked together to restore the original landscape of open, semidesert grasslands and bring back the native wildlife that once called these lush prairies home.
</desc>
