  <wpt lon="-111.15125664341423" lat="31.41977962383872">
    <name>Atascosa Mountains / Tumacacori Highlands</name>
    <cmt>Along Ruby Road, you will be riding through the otherworldy and dramatic landscape of the Atascosa Mountains to the north and the Pajaritos Mountains (and Pajarito Wilderness) to the south. The Atascosa and the Pajarito are smaller ranges that are part of the Tumacacori Highlands. The Tumacacori Highlands are part of a regional conservancy study of "travel corridors" for cats, called Cuatro Gatos, Four Cats, for mountain lions, ocelots, bobcats, and jaguars.  </cmt>
    <desc>Along Ruby Road, you will be riding through the otherworldy and dramatic landscape of the Atascosa Mountains to the north and the Pajaritos Mountains (and Pajarito Wilderness) to the south. The Atascosa and the Pajarito are smaller ranges that are part of the Tumacacori Highlands. The Tumacacori Highlands are part of a regional conservancy study of "travel corridors" for cats, called Cuatro Gatos, Four Cats, for mountain lions, ocelots, bobcats, and jaguars.  </desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>Dot</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lon="-111.18970315244476" lat="31.430774455084386">
    <name>Pajarito Wilderness / Sycamore Canyon</name>
    <cmt>Pajarita is a Spanish word meaning little bird. It is an appropriate name for this 7,420-acre area for several reasons. For one, the international border with Mexico forms the area's southern boundary. For another, the area's rugged canyons, which point south toward the subtropical environments of Mexico and Central America, provide a natural migration route for a surprising diversity of birds. The lush riparian habitat of Sycamore Canyon, the area's most prominent natural feature, also supports a number of resident species. As many as 160 species of birds have been observed in and around Sycamore Canyon. Though the area's rolling hills are predominantly covered with desert savannas and oak woodlands, its riparian areas are home to an astonishingly diverse vegetative community. Over 600 species of plants, some extremely rare, have been identified in these productive habitats. (Source: https://www.fs.usda.gov/recarea/coronado/recarea/?recid=25594)</cmt>
    <desc>Pajarita is a Spanish word meaning little bird. It is an appropriate name for this 7,420-acre area for several reasons. For one, the international border with Mexico forms the area's southern boundary. For another, the area's rugged canyons, which point south toward the subtropical environments of Mexico and Central America, provide a natural migration route for a surprising diversity of birds. The lush riparian habitat of Sycamore Canyon, the area's most prominent natural feature, also supports a number of resident species. As many as 160 species of birds have been observed in and around Sycamore Canyon. Though the area's rolling hills are predominantly covered with desert savannas and oak woodlands, its riparian areas are home to an astonishingly diverse vegetative community. Over 600 species of plants, some extremely rare, have been identified in these productive habitats. (Source: https://www.fs.usda.gov/recarea/coronado/recarea/?recid=25594)</desc>
    <sym>Dot</sym>
    <type>Dot</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lon="-111.23633335532713" lat="31.45995432776234">
    <name>Ruby Ghost Town</name>
    <cmt>Ruby is one of Arizona's best-preserved mining ghost towns. It includes 25 buildings, including the jail, houses, school, playground, and mine machinery, among many others. Ruby is entirely on private property, and there is a charge for admission to the site. 

Ruby was founded as a mining town in Bear Valley, named initially Montana Camp because the miners were mining at the foot of Montana Peak. Mining started circa 1877. The Montana Mine produced gold, silver, lead, zinc, and copper. At its peak in the mid-1930s, Ruby had a population of about 1,200. On April 11, 1912, the mining camp's general store owner Julius Andrews established the post office. Andrews named the post office "Ruby," after his wife, Lille B. Ruby Andrews, and the mining camp soon became known as Ruby. The most prosperous period for Ruby was in the late 1920s and 1930s when the Eagle-Picher Mining Company operated the mine and upgraded the camp. From 1934 to 1937, the Montana mine was Arizona's leading lead and zinc producer. In 1936, it was third in silver production. The mine closed in 1940, and Ruby was abandoned by the end of 1941. 

When the Mexican Revolution erupted in 1910, the town's proximity to the international border opened it up to harassment from bandits and rebels on many occasions. Between 1920 and 1922, the town of Ruby and the surroundings were the scenes of three double homicides known as the Ruby Murders, which led to the largest manhunt in the history of the Southwest, which included the first airplane ever used in an Arizona manhunt.
</cmt>
    <desc>Ruby is one of Arizona's best-preserved mining ghost towns. It includes 25 buildings, including the jail, houses, school, playground, and mine machinery, among many others. Ruby is entirely on private property, and there is a charge for admission to the site. 

Ruby was founded as a mining town in Bear Valley, named initially Montana Camp because the miners were mining at the foot of Montana Peak. Mining started circa 1877. The Montana Mine produced gold, silver, lead, zinc, and copper. At its peak in the mid-1930s, Ruby had a population of about 1,200. On April 11, 1912, the mining camp's general store owner Julius Andrews established the post office. Andrews named the post office "Ruby," after his wife, Lille B. Ruby Andrews, and the mining camp soon became known as Ruby. The most prosperous period for Ruby was in the late 1920s and 1930s when the Eagle-Picher Mining Company operated the mine and upgraded the camp. From 1934 to 1937, the Montana mine was Arizona's leading lead and zinc producer. In 1936, it was third in silver production. The mine closed in 1940, and Ruby was abandoned by the end of 1941. 

When the Mexican Revolution erupted in 1910, the town's proximity to the international border opened it up to harassment from bandits and rebels on many occasions. Between 1920 and 1922, the town of Ruby and the surroundings were the scenes of three double homicides known as the Ruby Murders, which led to the largest manhunt in the history of the Southwest, which included the first airplane ever used in an Arizona manhunt.
</desc>
