  <wpt lon="-111.33197087615976" lat="31.575217155805184">
    <name>Village of Arivaca </name>
    <cmt>Arivaca is a small community of artists, makers, growers, and aid workers with a grocery store, cantina, cafe, and mercantile. In addition, Arivaca hosts a farmers market every Saturday morning, highlighting some of the community's unique local art, crafts, and food.

Between 300 BC and 1400 AD, the Hohokam Peoples occupied the present-day Arivaca region. In 1400 AD, The O'odham (Pima and Tonono) Peoples, possibly descendants of the Hohokam, occupied the valley for many years and named the area; "Ari" (Ali) means "little," "vaca" (wahia) refers to a place where water comes up. Early Spaniards recorded the name as Aribac.

Due to the increased traffic of migrants and refugees crossing the border, the humanitarian aid group, No More Deaths began working in the town in 2004. Some have considered the town of Arivaca as an epicenter of efforts in solidarity with migrants and refugees, which has always put it in direct conflict with Border Patrol and has led to Arivaca becoming more heavily militarized and surveilled over the years. 
</cmt>
    <desc>Arivaca is a small community of artists, makers, growers, and aid workers with a grocery store, cantina, cafe, and mercantile. In addition, Arivaca hosts a farmers market every Saturday morning, highlighting some of the community's unique local art, crafts, and food.

Between 300 BC and 1400 AD, the Hohokam Peoples occupied the present-day Arivaca region. In 1400 AD, The O'odham (Pima and Tonono) Peoples, possibly descendants of the Hohokam, occupied the valley for many years and named the area; "Ari" (Ali) means "little," "vaca" (wahia) refers to a place where water comes up. Early Spaniards recorded the name as Aribac.

Due to the increased traffic of migrants and refugees crossing the border, the humanitarian aid group, No More Deaths began working in the town in 2004. Some have considered the town of Arivaca as an epicenter of efforts in solidarity with migrants and refugees, which has always put it in direct conflict with Border Patrol and has led to Arivaca becoming more heavily militarized and surveilled over the years. 
</desc>
